@page "/workflow"

<CatHead Title="Workflow">
    A run through a process, drawn as a diagram: what has happened, what is happening now, what comes
    next, and which way each decision went. Every node is markup the app writes, so a node can carry
    anything else in the library &mdash; an orb, a confidence, a progress bar, the caller's words. The
    wires between them are drawn from the structure and take their state from the step they lead into,
    so the app writes one attribute per step and nothing at all on a wire. No script.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Run" Title="A run" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <code>ol.workflow</code> is the run, each <code>li.workflow-step</code> one step holding a
        <code>.workflow-node</code>, and an <code>li.workflow-fork</code> a place where the run splits into
        <code>.workflow-branch</code>es, each with its own <code>ol.workflow-path</code>. The structure is
        the diagram: the list a screen reader walks is the same list the wires are drawn from.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The trail is the story.</strong> Wires behind the step at work are solid go, the wire into it
        carries a moving light, and the ones ahead are quiet &mdash; so a reader sees where the run has been
        and where it is without reading a node. A diagram wider than its container scrolls, so it takes
        <code>tabindex="0"</code> for the keyboard, and <code>data-follow</code> keeps the step at work in
        view: the diagram scrolls to it as states change, lets go when the reader scrolls away, and takes
        up again when they scroll back.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.States" Title="The state of a step" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-state</code> on the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> &mdash; <code>done</code>,
        <code>waiting</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>next</code> or
        <code>skipped</code>, and nothing at all for a step not reached yet. Put it on the step, never the
        node: the wire into the step reads it too. <code>waiting</code> is a step that has been reached and
        is held up by someone outside the run &mdash; a caller answering, a person approving &mdash; which is
        a different thing to tell a reader from a step still working.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The paint is not the words.</strong> A screen reader hears the list, not the tick, so say the
        state in the meta line or in a <code>.visually-hidden</code> span, as the first example does.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Looks" Title="The step at work" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A step at work wears one of three looks, chosen on its node: its edge in the light&rsquo;s colour
        with a ring, by default; <code>.workflow-node--glow</code>, the same edge with a glow; or
        <code>.workflow-node--agent</code>, the agent&rsquo;s two colours and glow, for a step an agent is
        doing. Put the class on the node whatever its state &mdash; the look shows only while the step is
        at work, so an app writes it once, from what the step is, and never from where the run is.
    </p>
    <p>
        The wires never wear the agent&rsquo;s colours. What runs along them is data, whichever kind of step
        sent it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Node" Title="What a node carries" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.workflow-node-head</code> is the one fixed part: whatever leads it &mdash; a
        <code>.workflow-node-icon</code> tile, an <a href="/orb"><code>.orb</code></a>, an
        <a href="/avatar"><code>.avatar</code></a> &mdash; then <code>.workflow-node-title</code> and
        <code>.workflow-node-meta</code>. A <code>.series-*</code> class on the tile colours it, and
        <code>.workflow-node-icon--agent</code> paints it in the agent's colours, so the steps an agent
        takes stand apart from the ones a tool does.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything after the head is the node's body, spaced for you: <code>.workflow-node-now</code> for
        what it is doing in a line or two &mdash; with <a href="/agent-status"><code>.text-shimmer</code></a>
        while the words are still a guess &mdash; a <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a>, chips, a
        <a href="/agent-status"><code>.confidence</code></a>, and <code>.workflow-node-foot</code> for the
        small print. Every wire meets the head's centre line, so nodes of any height still join in a
        straight line.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Parallel" Title="Steps that run side by side" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <code>.workflow-fork</code> followed by a step joins again: the bracket on the right gathers its
        branches into the next step, and a short branch's wire runs on to meet it. Nothing is written on a
        bracket. The piece down to each branch lights once that branch has been reached, and the piece
        back up once its last step is done, so a reader can see which branch the join is still waiting for.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Decision" Title="A decision" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.workflow-fork--decision</code> is a fork where one branch is chosen. Mark it
        <code>data-state="taken"</code> and the others <code>data-state="skipped"</code>: a skipped branch
        goes quiet, dashed and grey, with no state written on any step inside it. A
        <code>.workflow-label</code> first in a branch is the case it is for, on its wire.
    </p>
    <p>
        A fork that ends its path does not join; each branch ends where its last step does. Before the
        choice is made, leave both branches unmarked.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Loop" Title="Steps that repeat" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>li.workflow-loop</code> holds the path that repeats &mdash; a turn of a conversation, a retry
        &mdash; and draws the way back under it. <code>.workflow-loop-count</code> on the way back says how
        many times, in your words. <code>data-state</code> on the loop colours the way back, and the steps
        inside take their own: show the turn in progress, not every turn so far.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Labels" Title="Starts, ends and what was handed on" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.workflow-end</code> stands in for a node where a run starts or stops; done, it turns go. A
        <code>.workflow-label</code> first in a step is what was passed along the wire into it &mdash; a
        transcript, a ticket number, three articles &mdash; which is often the one thing a reader wants to
        know about a hand-off.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Down" Title="Top to bottom" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.workflow--down</code> runs the diagram down the page for a phone, a tablet held upright or a side
        panel. Nodes take the width they are given, and the wire runs down a rail through their icons. A fork
        hangs its branches off the rail, indented like a tree, and the rail runs on past them to the next
        step; a loop draws its way back up its right-hand side. The markup is the same as across &mdash; only
        the class differs.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.workflow--auto</code> is across on a wide screen and down below 640px, so one diagram serves a
        desk and a phone; the <a href="/workflow-run">live run</a>&rsquo;s detail uses it. With
        <code>data-follow</code> in a box of fixed height, a diagram run down keeps the step at work in view
        by scrolling down instead of across.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Flow" Title="How much moves" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One streak of light at a time leaves the step before the one at work and runs the whole way into it
        &mdash; down a fork&rsquo;s bracket into every branch at work, up a join&rsquo;s bracket from every
        branch into the step after it &mdash; at one speed, round every bend, in one piece. The step at work
        is what the reader watches, so the light stays quiet. <code>.workflow--flow-off</code> stills it to
        a lit line.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>--workflow-flow-speed</code> makes it slower or faster (<code>0.5</code> is half the pace) and
        <code>--workflow-flow-packets</code> sparser or denser at the same pace (<code>2</code> is twice as
        many). Set them on the diagram. The <a href="/workflow-run">live run</a> has a control for each.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Stages" Title="An overview" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same classes at another height. A handful of stages, each standing for a diagram of its own, is
        how a run reads to someone who will never open a step: <code>.workflow--lg</code> sizes everything
        up for a room, and <code>.workflow--fill</code> shares the width out so the overview fits instead of
        scrolling. <code>.workflow--sm</code> is the other way, for a diagram in a panel, and
        <code>.workflow--xl</code> further still, for a wall screen. A single node can be widened with
        <code>--workflow-node</code> on its step.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.workflow--focus</code> is for showing rather than reading: every step keeps only its head
        except the one at work, which opens wider with everything in it. Write the detail into every node;
        the diagram shows it for the step that is happening. The <a href="/workflow-run">wall screen</a> is
        the two together.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Release" Title="A run that is no agent's" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Nothing in a workflow is about agents. A release, a production line, a delivery, an onboarding
        &mdash; any run of steps with states is drawn the same way. The trail and the light are knobs on the
        <code>.workflow</code>: <code>--workflow-trail</code> for the way the run went, and
        <code>--workflow-flow</code>, <code>--workflow-flow-line</code>, <code>--workflow-flow-tint</code> and
        <code>--workflow-flow-glow</code> for the data flowing into the step at work and that step&rsquo;s
        edge. Set them from tokens, as here in the brand&rsquo;s colours.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Why" Title="Why it chose" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>ol.workflow-decision</code> is the options a step weighed, a <a href="/bars"><code>.meter</code></a>
        each, the score, and <code>.workflow-decision-why</code> under it. <code>data-state="chosen"</code>
        ticks the winner; <code>"rejected"</code> quiets the rest but keeps them readable, because why the
        others lost is half of the explanation. Pair it with the <a href="/agent-steps"><code>.reasoning</code></a>
        behind it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Workflow.Detail" Title="A step and what it did" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A node that opens its detail is a <code>&lt;button class="workflow-node"&gt;</code> with spans inside,
        and <code>aria-pressed="true"</code> on the one on show. The detail is your own panel beside the
        diagram &mdash; a <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-split</code></a> stacks it underneath when the room
        runs out &mdash; holding whatever explains the step: what it read, what it weighed, the tools it ran.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.workflow--grid</code> dots the canvas and <code>.workflow--center</code> centres a diagram
        narrower than its container. For a graph too large to write by hand &mdash; a flow generated from an
        automation's own definition &mdash; the <a href="/graph-gallery">graph</a> draws the same states on a
        canvas.
    </p>
</CatExample>
